- Style-library techniques-endings.md §T238: agrees verbatim on mechanics, window (P13 @ 75–81 s), evidence (ev/t078-pestle-mash.jpg · clips/pestle-mash.mp4). Tier DESCRIBED — OWED #105. No digest↔register disagreement.
- **Firsthand corrections/priced readings (binding on the build):** (1) The window's first ≈1.5 s is NOT mashing: census CP0/CP1 (75.0/76.0 s) are the wide shot closing the previous technique (T14 chess seasoning) — the bowl stands full of fully readable objects with the pestle AT REST on the table; the close-up begins between 76.0 and 76.5 s (CP at 76.5 s) with the pestle entering raised. The build therefore carries a SETUP act (0–1500 ms: full readable set, pestle at rest → raised) before stroke 1 — segment-anchored, not discarded (contrast T233's allocation, where the prior tail was dropped; here the full-object state IS this technique's required before-state, so it stays in the build). (2) The green flesh is present from the start as 3 large avocado-half slabs; the "paste" is those slabs progressively SPREADING to cover the bowl while the hard objects sink/deform — paste coverage growth and object identity loss are two coupled but distinct motions, both priced in the Plan. (3) Readable-object counts (builder's firsthand census count, approximate ±2): ≈28 at 0–1500 ms · ≈20 at 2000 ms · ≈18 at 3000 ms · ≈10 at 4000 ms · ≈6 at 5000 ms · ≈4 flecks at 5500 ms · ≈3 flecks at 6000 ms. The collapse is back-loaded in coverage but monotonic in count.

